{"cuisine/cuisine-of-hawaii":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-hawaii","n":"Hawaiian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of Hawaii incorporates five distinct styles of food, reflecting the diverse food history of settlement and immigration in the Hawaiian Islands, primarily originating from Polynesian, North American and East Asian cuisines.[a]"],["In the pre-contact period of Ancient Hawaii, Polynesian voyagers brought plants and animals to the Islands. As Native Hawaiians settled the area, they fished, raised taro for poi, planted coconuts, sugarcane, sweet potatoes and yams, and cooked meat and fish in earth ovens."],["After first contact in 1778, European and American cuisine arrived along with missionaries and whalers, who introduced their foods and built large sugarcane plantations. Christian missionaries brought New England cuisine while whalers introduced salted fish which eventually transformed into lomilomi salmon."],["As pineapple and sugarcane plantations grew, so did the demand for labor, bringing many immigrant groups to the Islands between 1850 and 1930. Immigrant workers brought cuisines from China, Korea, Japan, the Philippines, Puerto Rico and Portugal after arriving in Hawaii, introducing their new foods and influencing the region."],["The introduction of new ethnic foods, such as Chinese Cantonese char siu bao (manapua), Portuguese sweet bread and malasadas, Puerto Rican pasteles, and the Japanese bento, combined with the existing indigenous, European and American foods in the plantation working environments and the local communities."],["This blend of cuisines formed a \"local food\" style unique to Hawaii, resulting in plantation foods like the plate lunch, snacks like Spam musubi, and dishes like the loco moco. Shortly after World War II several well-known local restaurants opened their doors to serve \"Hawaiian Food\". Chefs further refined the local style and labeled it \"Hawaii regional cuisine\" in 1991, a style of cooking that makes use of locally grown ingredients to blend all of Hawaii's historical influences together to form a new fusion cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-hawaii"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Hawaii"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Butter mochi","dish/butter-mochi"],["Evil jungle prince","dish/evil-jungle-prince"],["Poi (Hawaiian food)","dish/poi-hawaiian-food"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Onolicious Hawaii","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"onolicioushawaii.com","note":"Hawaii-based blog of local-style recipes.","url":"https://onolicioushawaii.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Aloha Kitchen: Recipes from Hawai'i","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Alana Kysar, 2019","note":"Hawaii local food from poke to plate lunch, by a Maui-raised food blogger.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780399581366","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780399581366"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Mochiko","ingredient/mochiko"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"],["Swamp taro","ingredient/swamp-taro"],["Taro","ingredient/taro"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/78/Hawaiian-dishes-01.jpg/500px-Hawaiian-dishes-01.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"takaokun","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hawaiian-dishes-01.jpg"}},"cuisine/rajasthani-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/rajasthani-cuisine","n":"Rajasthani cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Rajasthani cuisine is the traditional cuisine of the Rajasthan state in north-west India. It was influenced by various factors like the warlike lifestyles of its inhabitants, the availability of ingredients in an arid region, and by Hindu temple traditions of sampradayas like Pushtimarg and Ramanandi. Food that could last for several days and could be eaten without heating was preferred."],["Scarcity of water and fresh green vegetables have all had their effect on the cooking. Signature Rajasthani dishes include dal baati churma, panchratna dal, papad ro saag, ker sangri, and gatte ro saag. It is also known for its snacks like bikaneri bhujia, mirchi bada and kanda kachauri. Other famous dishes include malaidar special lassi (lassi) and Lashun ki chutney (hot garlic paste), Mawa lassi from Jodhpur, Alwar ka mawa, Malpauas from Pushkar and rasgulla from Bikaner, \"paniya\"and \"gheriya\" from Mewar."],["Originating for the Marwar region of the state is the concept Marwari Bhojnalaya, or vegetarian restaurants, today found in many parts of India, which offer vegetarian food of the Marwari people. The history also has its effect on the diet as the Rajputs preferred majorly a non-vegetarian diet while the Brahmins, Jains, Bishnois and others preferred a vegetarian diet. So, the state has a myriad of both types of delicacies."],["According to a 2014 survey released by the Registrar General of India, Rajasthan has 74.9% vegetarians, which makes it the most vegetarian state in India."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"rajasthani-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rajasthani_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"],["Katt bafla","dish/katt-bafla"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"My Ginger Garlic Kitchen (Rajasthani)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mygingergarlickitchen.com","note":"Rajasthani section of Anupama Paliwal's Indian home cooking blog.","url":"https://www.mygingergarlickitchen.com/categories/rajasthani-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Tarla Dalal (Sindhi)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cdn.tarladalal.com","note":"Rajasthani category on the recipe site of the late Indian cookbook author Tarla Dalal.","url":"https://cdn.tarladalal.com/recipes/category/Rajasthani-Food","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Dal","ingredient/dal"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Jaggery","ingredient/jaggery"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Rajasthani cooking","count":1,"links":[["Vansh cuisine","cuisine/vansh-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/5/51/The_delicious_Rajasthani_food.png","w":500,"h":612,"by":"Master Sahil Gaikwad","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_delicious_Rajasthani_food.png"}},"cuisine/neapolitan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/neapolitan-cuisine","n":"Neapolitan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Neapolitan cuisine has influences that date back to the Greco-Roman period. Over the following centuries, the cuisine developed as different cultures controlled Naples and its kingdoms, such as those of Aragon and France."],["Because Naples was the capital of the Kingdom of Naples, its cuisine drew substantially from the cuisine of the entire Campania region, leading to the cuisine including both dishes based on rural ingredients (pasta, vegetables, cheese) and seafood (fish, crustaceans, mollusks). Many recipes are influenced by the local aristocratic cuisine, such as timballo and the sartù di riso, pasta or rice dishes with elaborate preparation, and dishes from popular traditions prepared with inexpensive but nutritious ingredients, such as pasta e fagioli (pasta and beans) and other pasta dishes with vegetables."],["Dishes originating from Neapolitan cuisine, particularly pasta and pizza, are among the best known foods in Italian cuisine worldwide, disseminated via migration. Citing this influence, food writer Waverley Root wrote in the late 60s that \"what the world knows as “Italian” cooking originated in [Naples].\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"neapolitan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neapolitan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Casatiello","dish/casatiello"],["Crocchè","dish/crocche"],["Pastiera","dish/pastiera"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Naples at Table: Cooking in Campania","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Arthur Schwartz, 1998","note":"Detailed guide to the home cooking of Naples and Campania.","url":"https://openroadmedia.com/ebook/naples-at-table/9780062319135","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Cracklings","ingredient/cracklings"],["Salami","ingredient/salami"],["Sourdough","ingredient/sourdough"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Brown sauce","ingredient/brown-sauce"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Shellfish","ingredient/shellfish"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/57/Neapolitan_pizza_at_Trappica_%2848701940197%29.jpg/500px-Neapolitan_pizza_at_Trappica_%2848701940197%29.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Bex Walton from London, England","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neapolitan_pizza_at_Trappica_(48701940197).jpg"}},"dish/bobotie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bobotie","n":"Bobotie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Bobotie is a South African dish consisting of spiced minced meat baked with an egg-based topping. It is not however a national dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bobotie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobotie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Afrikaner cuisine","cuisine/afrikaner-cuisine"],["South African cuisine","cuisine/south-african-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Drizzle and Dip","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"drizzleanddip.com","note":"South African recipes section of Cape Town food writer Sam Linsell's blog.","url":"https://drizzleanddip.com/category/recipes/cuisine/south-african/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"TASTE","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"taste.co.za","note":"South African recipe collection from the Woolworths food magazine.","url":"https://taste.co.za/south-african-recipes","suggested":true,"ki