"suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Savas Kitchen: Ukrainian recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"savaskitchen.com","note":"Ukrainian-born home cook Nataliia Savas's section of family Ukrainian recipes.","url":"https://savaskitchen.com/category/ukrainian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Estonian cuisine","links":[["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Egg butter","dish/egg-butter"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/97/Mint_bread_kvas.jpg/500px-Mint_bread_kvas.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mr.Icon (Mricon)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mint_bread_kvas.jpg"}},"ingredient/meat-floss":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/meat-floss","n":"Meat floss","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Meat floss, also known as  yuk sung or rousong, is a dried meat product of Chinese origin, with a light and fluffy texture similar to coarse cotton. It is more commonly known as bak hu (Hokkien: 肉拊, Pe̍h-ōe-jī: bah-hú) in Hokkien-influenced regions, such as Southeast Asia and Taiwan. Meat floss is golden in color with a distinctive flavor and sweet taste that is somewhat comparable to beef jerky."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-meat-floss"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meat_floss"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Lemper","dish/lemper"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Red onion","ingredient/red-onion"],["Serundeng","ingredient/serundeng"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/f/f4/Fragrance_Pork_Floss.jpg","w":500,"h":751,"by":"w:en:User:Khooeddi","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragrance_Pork_Floss.jpg"}},"dish/nimono":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nimono","n":"Nimono","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nimono is a simmered dish in Japanese cuisine. A nimono generally consists of a base ingredient simmered in shiru stock and seasoned with sake, soy sauce, mirin and a small amount of sweetening. The nimono is simmered in the shiru over a period of time until the liquid is absorbed into the base ingredient or evaporated. The base ingredient for a nimono is typically a vegetable, fish, seafood, or tofu, or some combination of these. The shiru stock for a nimono is generally dashi. Other than sake and soy sauce, the stock can be further flavored with mirin, sugar, salt, vinegar, miso, or other condiments."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nimono"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nimono"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Dashi","ingredient/dashi"],["Mirin","ingredient/mirin"],["Sake","ingredient/sake"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","li